---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Sopsdeck — a visual browser and editor for your secrets"
  description="A local UI for SOPS-encrypted secrets. Rename keys across your Project, find unused secrets, and browse secret history. Open source, with SOPS and age encryption."
  active="product"
  showDevBanner={true}
>
  <main class="landing">
    <section class="landing-hero" aria-labelledby="landing-title">
      <p class="product-label"><span aria-hidden="true"></span> A local interface for SOPS</p>
      <h1 id="landing-title">A visual browser and editor for <span>your secrets.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-description">
        Manage SOPS-encrypted dotenv, JSON, and YAML in your browser. Rename keys across your
        Project, find unused secrets, and keep changes in Git.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="#start"
          >Install Sopsdeck <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
        <a class="button ghost" href="#features">Explore the features</a>
      </div>
      <ul class="hero-facts" aria-label="Availability">
        <li>Free &amp; open source</li>
        <li>Runs locally</li>
        <li>macOS, Windows &amp; Linux</li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="editor-preview">
        <img
          src="/assets/landing-editor.jpg"
          width="1440"
          height="550"
          fetchpriority="high"
          alt="Sopsdeck's local editor with a Project file tree, three hidden secret values, an unused-secret badge, and access controls"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Your files on disk. A browser to work with them. Fictional demo secrets shown.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="encryption-adoption" aria-labelledby="adoption-title">
      <div class="adoption-intro">
        <h2 id="adoption-title">Built on SOPS and age.</h2>
        <p>The encryption tools behind real infrastructure workflows.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="adoption-examples">
        <a href="https://getsops.io/docs/security/#operational-requirements">
          <span class="adoption-name">Mozilla <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span>
          <span>Created SOPS to distribute secrets to EC2 infrastructure.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="https://fluxcd.io/flux/guides/mozilla-sops/">
          <span class="adoption-name">Flux <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span>
          <span>Uses SOPS with age to keep Kubernetes secrets encrypted in Git.</span>
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://indico.cern.ch/event/1106990/contributions/4991268/attachments/2530795/4354383/acat2022.pdf"
        >
          <span class="adoption-name">CERN · CMSWEB <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span>
          <span>Documents SOPS and age for managing deployment secrets.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-features" id="features" aria-labelledby="features-title">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="features-title">
          SOPS encrypts your files.<br />Sopsdeck helps you work with them.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Keep the SOPS file format and your Git workflow. Add a visual editor that understands the
          rest of your Project.
        </p>
      </div>

      <article class="feature-row">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <h3>Rename the secret.<br />Update the code.</h3>
          <p>
            Change a key in the editor and choose to rewrite its references across your Project.
            Sopsdeck finds whole-word references like <code>KEY</code>, <code>$KEY</code>, and
            <code>{'${KEY}'}</code>.
          </p>
          <p>Review the rename, opt into updating references, then encrypt and save.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/guide.html#rename-keys">
            See how renaming works <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <figure class="feature-visual">
          <img
            src="/assets/feature-rename.jpg"
            width="470"
            height="282"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="Sopsdeck's save preview renames STRIPE_SECRET to STRIPE_API_KEY with Rewrite references checked"
          />
          <figcaption>A rename, with reference updates you control.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="feature-row feature-row--reverse">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <h3>Find the secrets<br />you stopped using.</h3>
          <p>
            Old integrations leave keys behind. Sopsdeck scans your Project and marks keys with zero
            references as unused, right beside the value.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treat the badge as a prompt to investigate. Dynamic references or tools outside the
            Project may still use a key. Nothing is deleted automatically.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/guide.html#unused-secrets">
            Understand the scan <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <figure class="feature-visual unused-visual">
          <img
            src="/assets/feature-unused.jpg"
            width="390"
            height="210"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="STRIPE_SECRET and DATABASE_URL have references, while LEGACY_API_TOKEN has an unused badge. All values are hidden."
          />
          <figcaption>Spot a leftover key without revealing its value.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <div class="workflow-comparison">
        <div>
          <h3>What Sopsdeck adds</h3>
          <p>
            SOPS already provides encryption, recipients, and selective field encryption. Sopsdeck
            adds these Project workflows on top.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/guide.html"
            >Read the guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
          >
        </div>
        <div
          class="comparison-scroll"
          tabindex="0"
          role="region"
          aria-label="SOPS and Sopsdeck feature comparison"
        >
          <table>
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Built-in workflows in SOPS CLI and Sopsdeck</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr
                ><th scope="col">Workflow</th><th scope="col">SOPS CLI</th><th scope="col"
                  >Sopsdeck</th
                ></tr
              >
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Rename + rewrite Project references</th><td>Not built in</td><td
                  >Review &amp; save</td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Find keys with no Project references</th><td>Not built in</td><td
                  >Unused badges</td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Browse one secret's Git history</th><td>Manual steps</td><td
                  ><a href="/docs/guide.html#secret-history">Browse &amp; restore</a></td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Edit secrets in a browser</th><td>External editor</td><td
                  >Local visual editor</td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Encrypt files + choose recipients</th><td>Built in</td><td
                  >Uses SOPS</td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><th scope="row">Encrypt selected JSON / YAML fields</th><td>Built in</td><td
                  ><a href="/docs/guide.html#field-encryption">Choose in the UI</a></td
                ></tr
              >
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="encryption-explainer"
      id="encrypted-in-place"
      aria-labelledby="encryption-title"
    >
      <div>
        <h2 id="encryption-title">Your config stays config.</h2>
        <p>
          SOPS encrypts selected values in place. Key names and unselected fields stay readable, so
          a locked file still looks like your dotenv, JSON, or YAML.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keep encrypted files in Git, review changes, and share Access with a teammate's age public
          key. Each person keeps their own private key.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/guide.html#encrypted-in-place">
          See encrypted files and Access <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <figure class="code-visual">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <span>eas.json</span><span class="encrypted-label">Encrypted on disk</span>
        </div>
        <pre
          aria-label="Illustrative SOPS-encrypted JSON with one encrypted token"><code>{`{
  "cli": { "version": "20.5.1" },
  "build": {
    "env": {
      "EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL": "https://api.example.com",
      "EXPO_TOKEN": `}<span class="ciphertext">"ENC[AES256_GCM,…]"</span>{`
    }
  },
  "sops": { "age": [{ "recipient": "age1…" }] }
}`}</code></pre>
        <figcaption>Illustrative ciphertext and metadata, shortened for readability.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-security" id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
      <div class="security-intro">
        <p class="product-label"><span aria-hidden="true"></span> SOPS + age, explained</p>
        <h2 id="security-title">
          What encryption protects.<br />And what you still need to protect.
        </h2>
        <p>
          SOPS encrypts values with AES-256-GCM. With age recipients, age encrypts the file's data
          key so the matching private identities can unlock it. Sopsdeck uses these tools and stores
          your private age identity in the operating system keychain.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://getsops.io/docs/security/">
          Read the SOPS security documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="security-details">
        <details open>
          <summary>Someone gets a copy of an encrypted file</summary>
          <p>
            The encrypted values need an authorized private key to decrypt. Key names, structure,
            unencrypted fields, and recipient metadata remain visible. Keep encrypted files
            reasonably private, too.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Your private key or device is compromised</summary>
          <p>
            Encryption cannot protect values from someone who has your decryption key or can read
            them on an unlocked device. Protect your machine and keep a private-key backup in a
            password manager. Losing every usable key means losing access.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You remove someone from Access</summary>
          <p>
            Sopsdeck removes their recipient and rotates the file's SOPS data key. That protects the
            new version. It cannot take back copied values or old Git revisions they can still
            decrypt. Rotate the actual API keys or passwords they had access to.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You unlock a file for a local tool</summary>
          <p>
            Unlock writes plaintext to disk. That copy is readable by local tools and anyone with
            access to it. Lock it again when finished, and never commit the unlocked file. Hiding a
            value in the UI only hides it on screen.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>You need to trust a change or an automation</summary>
          <p>
            Encryption does not decide who should be trusted. A recipient who can decrypt can also
            copy or re-encrypt the data. Review recipients and file changes in Git, and protect the
            identities your automation uses. SOPS also supports cloud KMS access controls;
            Sopsdeck's team workflow uses age keys.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p class="security-footnote">
          A practical summary, with Sopsdeck-specific handling covered in
          <a href="/docs/guide.html#what-sopsdeck-stores">what Sopsdeck stores</a>. See the <a
            href="https://getsops.io/docs/security/#threat-model">full SOPS threat model</a
          >
          and the <a href="https://github.com/FiloSottile/age">age documentation</a> for details.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="start-title">Open your Project.<br />Get to your secrets.</h2>
        <p>Install with npm, then open a folder. SOPS is included. You need Node.js and Git.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/guide.html#get-started">
          Follow the setup guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="install-command">
        <div class="code-toolbar"><span>Terminal</span><span>macOS · Windows · Linux</span></div>
        <pre
          aria-label="Install and open Sopsdeck"><code><span class="code-comment"># Install in your Project</span>{`
npm install -D @sopsdeck/sopsdeck
npx sopsdeck .`}</code></pre>
        <p>The package downloads the matching local runner on first launch.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
